When my grandfather died, he left me two things: a box of photographs and a bicycle that had not been ridden for thirty years. The photographs I looked through that same evening, laughing and crying in roughly equal measure. The bicycle, rusting in the corner of his garage under a pile of old newspapers, I tried hard to ignore.

{1}

I knew nothing about bicycles, apart from how to ride one, and even that I had not done for years. My first step was to wheel it to a repair shop in town, a narrow room that smelled of oil and old leather, where the owner, a quiet man called Tomás, studied the frame for a long time before delivering his verdict.

{2}

So began the strangest apprenticeship of my life. Every Saturday I arrived at eight, and every Saturday Tomás found something new for me to take apart. He rarely explained anything; instead, he would watch me struggle for a while, sipping his coffee, and then, with a single movement, show me what I had been doing wrong.

{3}

The hardest part of the whole project was the paint. The original colour, a deep racing green, had faded almost to grey, and none of the samples in the hardware shop came anywhere close. I tried three of them on an old piece of metal, and each one looked worse than the last.

{4}

It took another two months to finish the work. By then I had replaced the chain, the brakes and both tyres, cleaned every spoke by hand and learned more about patience than in all my years at school. On the last Saturday, Tomás inspected everything twice, tested the brakes himself and gave a single, satisfied nod.

{5}

Those early problems were soon solved, and the bicycle now takes me to work most days. People sometimes stop me in the street to ask how old it is or where I found it, and I enjoy telling them its story, perhaps at slightly greater length than they had hoped. One elderly man even recognised the make and told me he had owned the same model as a boy.

{6}

I still go to the workshop on Saturday mornings, although these days Tomás lets me help with other people's bicycles. He says I am almost ready to work on my own. Coming from a man who rarely says anything at all, that is the highest praise imaginable.

I arrived in Lisbon on a Sunday evening in September with two suitcases, a phrasebook and the address of a flat I had only ever seen in photographs. The taxi driver, hearing my hesitant Portuguese, switched immediately to English, which I took as a bad sign. As the car climbed through the dark streets, I tried to remember why I had thought this was a good idea.

{1}

What stopped me was my flatmate, Inês, who appeared at the door with a plate of grilled sardines and an enormous smile. She was studying architecture, spoke four languages and seemed to know everyone in the neighbourhood. Within an hour, she had introduced me to the woman who ran the café downstairs and the elderly man who sold lottery tickets on the corner.

{2}

It took four days, eleven emails and a great deal of patience to sort out the problem. In the meantime, I had plenty of time to explore. I rode the old yellow trams up and down the hills, got lost in the narrow streets of Alfama and discovered that the city looks completely different at sunset, when the light turns the river gold. I also ate far too many custard tarts.

{3}

Inês was less sympathetic than I had hoped. 'Nobody understands that professor,' she told me cheerfully. 'Even the Portuguese students record his lectures.' She suggested I did the same, and that evening she sat with me for an hour, explaining the expressions I had missed.

{4}

By the end of the week, I had a student card, a timetable and the beginnings of a routine. The lottery seller on the corner greeted me by name, and the woman at the café knew how I liked my coffee. I had also, without quite noticing, stopped translating everything in my head before I spoke.

{5}

I made dozens of mistakes that night, and nobody seemed to mind in the slightest. Several people corrected me with great kindness, one wrote down a list of useful insults, and a girl from Porto invited me to visit her family at Christmas. By midnight I had laughed more than in the whole previous month.

{6}

I stayed for a full year in the end, and I have been back four times since. Inês and I still speak every week. My Portuguese is still far from perfect, but I can follow almost any lecture now, and I have never again needed to sit on a suitcase to think things over.

For most of its hundred-year history, Ashbury Town Football Club was the kind of club that only its own supporters had heard of. It played in a small stadium on the edge of the village, attracted crowds of a few hundred and spent most seasons in the lower half of the league. Its most famous moment was a cup run decades ago that ended in the third round.

{1}

The announcement caused uproar. Within hours, a meeting had been called in the village hall, and so many people turned up that the doors had to be left open so that those standing outside could hear what was being said. Some came straight from work in their uniforms; others brought their children, many of whom were wearing the club's faded blue shirt.

{2}

The idea spread faster than anyone could have predicted. Children emptied their piggy banks, a local bakery donated a week's takings, and a former player who had moved abroad sent a cheque with a note that simply said: 'For the club that gave me my start.' Even the village's oldest resident, aged 102, insisted on contributing.

{3}

In response, the campaign promised that the stadium would be opened up for the whole community during the week, with space for a youth club, a café and classes for older residents. Plans were drawn up by volunteers, and a local builder offered to do the work at cost. The promise won over many of the doubters.

{4}

Nobody has ever discovered who sent the money, although most people in the village have a theory. Some suspect the former player; others are convinced it was the owner himself, wanting to make amends. The club's new owners, two thousand of them, held their first annual meeting a month later.

{5}

Success has brought its own challenges, however. Higher divisions mean higher wages and longer journeys, and some members worry that the club is losing the character that made it special. Every decision now has to be put to a vote, and meetings can be long and heated. A few long-standing supporters have even stopped attending them.

{6}

As I left the ground that afternoon, a group of children was practising penalties in the shadow of the main stand, watched by a small crowd of grandparents. It was hard to imagine that, only a few years earlier, the whole place had been just weeks away from disappearing under a housing estate.

For years I told anyone who would listen that I would try stand-up comedy one day. Friends laughed at my stories at dinner parties, and I had convinced myself that being funny with people you know cannot be very different from being funny with a room full of strangers. My friends, to their credit, never once tried to talk me out of it. I was wrong about almost everything, as it turned out, but I am still glad I found out.

{1}

The first problem was material. Stories that worked brilliantly when I told them over a glass of wine seemed flat and pointless when I wrote them down. Worse, the funniest moments in those stories depended on knowing the people involved, which no audience would. I filled a notebook with ideas and crossed out almost all of them.

{2}

So I wrote about my driving test, which I failed four times, and about my disastrous attempts to learn salsa. I timed every joke with a stopwatch and cut anything that ran on too long. I practised in front of the bathroom mirror every evening until my flatmate began to complain about the noise.

{3}

By the night of the show, I had rewritten half the routine and was no longer sure that any of it was funny. The pub was busier than I had expected, my hands were shaking so much that I could barely hold my drink, and the comedian on before me was so good that I seriously considered slipping out of the back door.

{4}

Then something unexpected happened. I forgot a line, admitted as much to the audience, and the room burst out laughing. From that moment, everything felt easier. The driving test story went down well; the salsa story went down even better. Someone at the back even shouted out a question about my dancing.

{5}

I did go back the following month, and the month after that. Some nights went well; one or two were so bad that I still wince when I think about them. I learned which jokes worked with a Friday crowd and which only worked on a Tuesday. Gradually, though, I learned to enjoy the uncertainty.

{6}

I still have a day job, and I suspect I always will. But every few weeks I stand on a small stage in a crowded pub, and for five minutes I am somebody slightly braver than I am the rest of the time.
